  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nTropical storm Bertha will continue to track westward along the Gulf \nCoast towards the Texas coast. This will allow for an increase in \nconvection across the area, mainly along a corridor of enhanced \nCAPE,PWAT and dewpoint values as the southwesterly flow brings \ndeeper moisture into the CWA around the Tropical Storm. Isolated to \nscattered convection will be possible through this evening, with a \nfew stronger storms not entirely out of the question. Increasing \ncloud cover and convection will help limit the dangerous, keeping \nheat index values below product criteria. That said, heat indices \nwill peak our around 105 for most of the area, with portions along \nthe Mississippi River Valley potentially reaching slightly higher \nvalues nearing 108 degrees.. \n\nDangerous heat stress conditions will persist through the rest of \nthis week and into the weekend with heat values remaining around 105 \nto 110 degrees through Sunday. Expanding influence from the ridge \naloft will likely push us back into Elevated and Significant heat \nstress by early next week and into the mid week. Additional heat \nheadlines and advisories/warnings are likely to be needed as we \nget closer. Heat trends will continued to be monitored. Rain \nchances will begin to increase by next Thursday. /KP/CR/",
